{
    "background_story": "Donald, a curious and adventurous anthropologist, had always been fascinated by the rich cultural heritage of the Andean region. He spent years studying the Quechua language and traditions, eventually becoming fluent in the indigenous tongue. His passion led him to Peru, where he immersed himself in the local community, learning from the elderly wisdom-keepers and participating in ancient rituals. Donald's respect and dedication to the Quechua people earned him the title of 'Kusi' (happy one) among the villagers. He worked tirelessly to help preserve the Quechua language and customs, establishing a cultural center to promote cross-cultural understanding and exchange. Through his work, Donald became a bridge between two worlds, fostering a deeper appreciation for the Quechua heritage and its people.",
